+++ b/docs/scanner.md
@@ -0,0 +1,67 @@
+# From Scanner
+
+The native desktop toolbar, directly beside **E-Sign**, and the File menu offer
+**From Scanner → Scan / Manage presets**.
+The scanner windows and image-processing engine are supplied by the separately
+reusable, MIT-licensed [Rust OpenScan companion](https://github.com/kevin7du-cpu/openscan/tree/rust-portability/rust). Both applications use Rust and egui/eframe;
+the optional driver component communicates over a versioned JSON process protocol.
+
+Place the complete platform-specific OpenScan package in `scanner/` beside the PdfCraft executable, or
+set `PDFCRAFT_SCANNER` to its absolute executable path during development. The
+adapter never searches the working directory or PATH. No service, port or scanner
+operation starts merely by launching PdfCraft.
+
+Scan opens the acquisition/review window. Choose a scanner, paper source, color,
+size and resolution. Select cleanup/OCR options, acquire pages and save. The returned
+PDF opens through PdfCraft's normal background file-opening pipeline. Existing open
+documents are unchanged. Append writes a separate PDF copy, and originals remain
+in the companion's recovery session. Presets are managed in a separate window.
+
+The Rust companion supports installed Windows WIA drivers, installed SANE `scanimage`
+drivers on Linux/macOS, and eSCL/AirScan network devices on all three platforms.
+TWAIN and native macOS ICA are not implemented. Manufacturer drivers and physical
+feeder/duplex support are still required. Network mDNS discovery is an explicit
+action; normal driver refresh does not open a discovery listener. HTTPS certificate
+validation remains enabled. See the companion's platform setup and validation notes.
+
+Headless tools use the same adapter:
+
+- `scanner_devices`: list devices without acquiring a page.
+- `scanner_capabilities device_id=…`: available sources and resolution ranges.
+- `scanner_scan`: `profile` (OpenScan v1 object), new `path` (PDF), and new
+  `session_path` (recovery directory). These paths must pass automation root checks.
+- `scanner_presets`: list presets in `directory`.
+- `scanner_preset_save`: save `profile` in `directory`; explicit `overwrite=true`
+  is required for an existing name.
+- `scanner_preset_delete`: remove `name` from `directory`.
+
+Presets use `name`, `deviceId`, `source` (`flatbed`/`feeder`/`duplex`), `dpi` (75–600),
+`paper` (`letter`/`a4`/`legal`/`a5`), `color` (`color`/`grayscale`/`blackWhite`),
+`maxPages` (1–250), `searchablePdf`, `jpegQuality` and `cleanup`. Cleanup accepts
+`deskew`, `autoCrop`, `removeBackground`, `denoise`, `sharpen`, `skipBlankPages`,
+`blankInkPercent`, `brightness`, `contrast`, `rotateDegrees`. The companion validates
+values and rejects unknown fields. Vendor-native dialogs are not exposed by this implementation.
+
+The adapter enforces a 4 MB response limit and a 15-minute headless timeout.
+Interactive windows stay open until the user finishes. All driver work is off the
+PdfCraft UI thread. Companion errors become notices/errors rather than replacing
+an open document.
+
+Tests:
+
+```text
+cargo test -p pdfcraft-engine scanner_protocol
+cargo test -p pdfcraft-automation scanner_tools_refuse
+# With PDFCRAFT_SCANNER pointing to the built companion (no paper scan):
+cargo test -p pdfcraft-automation scanner_presets_round_trip -- --ignored
+cargo test -p pdfcraft-ui-egui --test scanner_toolbar
+```
+
+OpenScan's Rust tests cover correction, original preservation, presets, recovery,
+synthetic eSCL acquisition, multipage TIFF, PDF append, Unicode text-layer encoding,
+the process protocol and accessible egui controls. Real-model OCR was independently
+checked by extracting and rendering its PDF with PdfCraft. Windows HP Smart Tank
+7600 and fi-7030 driver discovery/capabilities passed; the Rust fi-7030 acquisition
+returned an empty feeder. A fresh physical page, duplex/HP acquisition, Linux/macOS
+native runtime checks and the companion's final native UI inspection remain pending.
+The older C# companion's successful hardware scan is not evidence for the Rust rewrite.
